    {
      "id": "005f57fe828aca2341cc6876744ecba2907c9989",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 599829795,
          "type": null,
          "body": "> Nobody was yet able to explain the point of enabling this warning or what purpose it serves.\n\nThis all started, I suspect, with [a mail message \"Idle Thought - Compiling with C++\"](https://www.wireshark.org/lists/wireshark-dev/201302/msg00056.html) in 2013.\n\nEd Beroset then submitted bug #8416, with a bunch of changes \"To clean up parts of Wireshark and to make it compatible with C++ compilers\".\n\nI then assumed *somebody* considered this important enough to make those changes, and added the flag so that incompatible changes wouldn't drift back in.\n\nThe two benefits listed in that message were:\n\n- \"Much more up-to-date Windows compiler (see the regular litany of complaints that is being stuck on C90 due to MSVC). As far as I can tell, Microsoft's C++ compiler is still actively developed and already supports some parts of C++11.\"\n- \"Built-in exceptions. This would be additional migration work, but it would permit us to drop a non-trivial amount of code that we currently own in order to do our own exceptions.\"\n\nThe former no longer appears to apply, as MSVC now gives enough C99 that we can rely on most if not all of the useful parts of it.\n\nThe latter might apply, although I don't know whether 1) there's significant additional overhead from C++ exceptions or 2) how much work it would entail.  (Note, BTW, that at least in some runs I made, reading in a really big file with Wireshark, a significant amount of time seemed to be spent in `setjmp()`.)\n\nAt this point, unless somebody *really* strongly advocates keeping that check in, I'd say remove it.  (I won't strongly advocate it....)\n\n> CMake agrees with me by the way:\n\nPresumably the intent was that files be converted to C++ *and renamed to .cpp*, so that we wouldn't be specifying a C++ compiler as the C compiler.\n\nSomebody doing that could turn the flag back on to see what needs to be fixed (over and above any changes made to make use of C++ features).",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-06-13T02:05:11.193Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-06-13T02:05:11.193Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 103886947,
